# =============================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE CONTRACT, and this file did not exist until a
# Windows CI leg proved it.
#
# MEASURED, run 31148233876, `checkers (windows-latest)`, step "gauge hook
# corpus": six rows failed with messages of the form
#
#     FAIL  row 1: hook 0.09 != corpus 0.09
#
# The two values are byte-DIFFERENT and render IDENTICALLY. GitHub's Windows
# runner checks out with `core.autocrlf=true`, so every line of
# `checker/gauge-corpus.tsv` arrived with a trailing CR, the last tab-separated
# field became `0.09\r`, and a string comparison against the hook's `0.09`
# failed. Ubuntu and macOS passed the same commit. The gate was correct: the
# strings really did differ. What it could not do was SHOW the difference.
#
# Three things were wrong and all three are fixed. This file is the first:
# the working tree is now LF on every platform, so the data a checker reads is
# the data that was committed. The second is in `checker/gauge-hook-corpus.sh`,
# which now strips CR when parsing and escapes invisible bytes in its failure
# message. The third is `checker/portability.sh`, which now refuses a tracked
# text file containing CRLF at all.
#
# Belt and braces on purpose. A contributor who clones with a different
# autocrlf setting, or an editor that rewrites a file, must not be able to turn
# a data file into a phantom mismatch -- and if one does, the message must name
# the byte instead of printing two identical-looking numbers.
# =============================================================================

# Every text file: normalised to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
# `eol=lf` is the half that matters here -- `text=auto` alone still hands
# Windows a CRLF checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Data a checker parses. Called out explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`
# heuristics: these are the files whose bytes a gate compares.
*.tsv  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Shell must be LF everywhere. A `.sh` with CRLF fails on Linux with
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M` -- a defect that reads like a missing
# interpreter and is really a line ending.
*.sh   text eol=lf

# PowerShell is CRLF-tolerant and is authored on Windows; leave it LF too so
# the two installer arms are byte-comparable.
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.zip  binary
*.png  binary
*.olean binary
